      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which basement drainage system is best?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most existing homes with a real groundwater problem, the best combination is fixed grading and gutters, an interior perimeter drain with cleanouts, and a sump pit with a backup pump. Exterior footing drains are better on new builds or when you are excavating anyway. Baseboard channels suit only leaking block walls over a dry floor."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does a basement drainage system cost?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Typical ranges run from almost nothing for downspout extensions, to roughly $6,000 to $15,000 for an interior perimeter drain on an average house, to $15,000 to $40,000 or more for exterior excavation. Prices vary widely by region, perimeter length, depth and access, so treat these as ballparks and compare at least three itemised quotes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a basement drainage system last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Well-built drain tile, interior or exterior, commonly lasts thirty years or more, and modern PVC in washed stone with filter fabric can last far longer. The pieces that wear out first are the sump pump, usually within seven to ten years, and the float switch. Clogging from silt or iron ochre shortens pipe life where there are no cleanouts."
          }
