        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a system before diagnosing the leak</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Contractors sell what they install, so the first quote often decides the system. Spend one storm watching where water appears first. Floor edge leaks point to an interior perimeter drain, face-of-wall seepage points elsewhere, and humidity points to a dehumidifier. Diagnosis is free, and the wrong system can cost ten thousand dollars.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping gutters and grading</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Downspouts dumping at the foundation and soil sloping toward the house cause a large share of wet basements. Fixing both costs little and often solves the problem outright. Installing an interior drain first means paying to pump roof water that should never have reached the backfill, and the sump runs harder for its whole life.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Accepting a drain with no cleanouts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every perimeter drain slowly collects silt, and in iron-rich soils iron ochre can choke pipe within a few years. Without cleanout ports at the corners, the only fix is breaking the slab open again. Insist on cleanouts in the quote and flush the lines when the sump pit starts showing orange sludge.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the sump pump as an afterthought</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The pump is the single point of failure for the whole system. A premium drain feeding a bargain pump with no backup, no alarm and a discharge line that dumps water back at the foundation is a flood waiting for a power cut. Budget for backup and replace the pump on schedule.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Tying groundwater drains into the sewer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Connecting a sump or perimeter drain to the sanitary sewer is prohibited in most municipalities because it overloads treatment plants during storms. It can also let sewage back into your drain tile when the main line surcharges. Discharge to the yard, a dry well or a permitted storm connection instead, and check local rules first.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which basement drainage system is best?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most existing homes with a real groundwater problem, the best combination is fixed grading and gutters, an interior perimeter drain with cleanouts, and a sump pit with a backup pump. Exterior footing drains are better on new builds or when you are excavating anyway. Baseboard channels suit only leaking block walls over a dry floor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a basement drainage system cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typical ranges run from almost nothing for downspout extensions, to roughly $6,000 to $15,000 for an interior perimeter drain on an average house, to $15,000 to $40,000 or more for exterior excavation. Prices vary widely by region, perimeter length, depth and access, so treat these as ballparks and compare at least three itemised quotes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a basement drainage system last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Well-built drain tile, interior or exterior, commonly lasts thirty years or more, and modern PVC in washed stone with filter fabric can last far longer. The pieces that wear out first are the sump pump, usually within seven to ten years, and the float switch. Clogging from silt or iron ochre shortens pipe life where there are no cleanouts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a French drain and weeping tile?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both are perforated pipe in gravel that collects groundwater. Weeping tile usually means the pipe laid outside along the footing during construction. A French drain is the broader term and, in basement work, often means the interior perimeter drain installed under the slab edge. Landscapers also use French drain for yard drainage trenches, which adds to the confusion.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a basement drainage system myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some parts, yes. Gutters, downspout extensions, regrading, baseboard channel kits and pump replacement are realistic DIY jobs. An interior perimeter drain means sawing and breaking concrete, removing tonnes of debris and re-pouring the slab, which is possible for a determined owner but rarely worth it. Exterior excavation to the footing is contractor work because of trench collapse risk.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my footing drain is clogged?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Warning signs include water appearing along the floor edge where it never used to, a sump pit that stays dry during heavy rain despite years of regular running, and orange slime or silt in the pit. A camera inspection through a cleanout or the pit inlet costs a few hundred dollars and shows whether the pipe is clogged, crushed or separated.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a basement drainage system stop dampness and humidity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not by itself. A drainage system handles liquid water moving through soil and under the slab. Humidity, condensation on cold walls and musty air come from moisture in the air and need dehumidification, ventilation and insulation. Many basements need both, but if your floor edges stay dry after storms, the drain is not your problem.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

