        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A basement drainage system is only as good as its diagnosis. Fix the gutters and grading, watch where the water enters during a real storm, and buy the system that intercepts it there, not the one the first contractor happens to install.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most houses with groundwater, that is an interior perimeter drain with cleanouts feeding a sump with a backup pump and an alarm. Spend the money on the drain and the pump, and skip the channel systems and upgrades that do not address where your water is actually coming from.
          </p>
